    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d818">

                <pb facs="1018" n="1004" sameAs="frus:frus1945v09/pg1004"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 PW/2–445: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Ecuador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Scotten</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Quito</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-02-04T10:00:00-05:00">February 4,
                            1945—10 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>100. This morning’s press publishes the announcement that Ecuador is in a state
                    of war with Japan (as foreseen in my No. 99 of February 3, 11 p.m.<anchor n="15"
                        xml:id="d818-f1"/>), reporting that President Velasco informed the press
                    last night that “the Government of Ecuador, duly authorized by the National
                    Constitutional Assembly, has declared that it has been in a state of war with
                    Japan since December 7, 1941, in order to demonstrate that Ecuador is always
                    with the United Nations, struggling against totalitarian tyranny”. The President
                    added that the text of the declaration would be made available to the press at
                    noon tomorrow.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Scotten</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
